# Astro 6 Best Practices

## Project Structure

- Follow the conventional `src/` layout: `pages/`, `components/`, `layouts/`, `lib/`, `middleware.ts`
- Place all routes inside `src/pages/` — file name = URL path
- Use `src/layouts/` for shared page wrappers (HTML shell, `<head>`, navigation)
- Keep reusable UI in `src/components/`; keep data-fetching logic in `src/lib/`
- Use `public/` only for static assets that must not be processed by Astro
- Prefer `astro add <integration>` over editing `package.json` directly for integrations

## Component Design

- Each `.astro` file has two parts separated by `---`: the **component script** (server-only JS) and the **component template** (HTML + expressions)
- All code in the frontmatter script (`---`) runs server-side only — never reaches the browser
- Access props via `const { title } = Astro.props`; define them with a TypeScript `interface Props` for type safety
- Use `<slot />` for default child injection and named slots (`<slot name="header" />`) for structured layouts
- Provide fallback content inside `<slot>` tags for when no children are passed
- Use `<Fragment slot="name">` to pass multiple elements into a named slot without a wrapper div
- Prefer composition over deeply nested components — small, focused components compose better

## SSR and On-Demand Rendering

- For fully dynamic apps (like PA Plan Advisor), set `output: 'server'` in `astro.config.mjs`
- In `server` mode, all pages are server-rendered by default — opt static pages in with `export const prerender = true`
- For hybrid sites, default is static — opt individual routes into SSR with `export const prerender = false`
- Always install the correct adapter for your deployment target via `astro add` (e.g. `astro add netlify`, `astro add node`)
- Use `Astro.request` to access the standard Web API `Request` object (url, headers, method, body)
- Use `Astro.response` to set response status and headers before returning
- Return `new Response(null, { status: 404 })` or `Astro.redirect('/path', 301)` directly from page frontmatter for early exits
- Use `Astro.cookies` for reading and writing cookies in SSR pages and API endpoints

## Routing and Pages

- Dynamic routes use bracket syntax: `src/pages/scenarios/[id].astro` → `/scenarios/:id`
- Access route params with `const { id } = Astro.params`
- API routes (server endpoints) live in `src/pages/api/` and export named HTTP method functions (`GET`, `POST`, etc.)
- API routes must return a `Response` object
- Use `export const prerender = false` on all auth-protected pages and API routes
- Group admin-only routes under `src/pages/admin/` and protect them in middleware

## Middleware

- Create `src/middleware.ts` to handle auth, role checks, and redirects for every request
- Use `defineMiddleware` from `astro:middleware` and call `next()` to continue the chain
- Read the session cookie and validate the JWT in middleware before reaching any protected page
- Redirect unauthenticated requests to `/login`; redirect unauthorized role access to `/dashboard`
- Attach user data to `locals` (`context.locals.user`) so pages receive it without re-fetching

```ts
// src/middleware.ts pattern
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro:middleware';

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  const token = context.cookies.get('session')?.value;
  if (!token && context.url.pathname !== '/login') {
    return context.redirect('/login');
  }
  return next();
});
```

## Authentication (Supabase Pattern)

- Use `supabase.auth.signInWithPassword()` and store the JWT in an `httpOnly` cookie — never in `localStorage`
- Validate the JWT server-side with `supabase.auth.getUser(token)` on every protected request
- Never store role information in the JWT claims — always query the role from the backend database
- Use API routes (`src/pages/api/auth/login.ts`, `logout.ts`) to set and clear session cookies
- Set `httpOnly: true`, `secure: true`, `sameSite: 'lax'` on all session cookies

## Integrations

- Use `astro add` for all official integrations (React, Tailwind, Netlify, Node, etc.)
- Enable React (or another framework) only for components that require client-side interactivity
- Mark interactive components with a `client:` directive: `client:load`, `client:idle`, `client:visible`
- `client:load` — hydrate immediately; use sparingly (increases JS bundle)
- `client:idle` — hydrate when browser is idle; good for non-critical interactive widgets
- `client:visible` — hydrate only when component enters viewport; best for below-the-fold content
- Never add a `client:` directive to components that do not need client-side interactivity

## Performance

- Astro sends zero JavaScript by default — only add `client:` directives when truly necessary
- Prefer static pre-rendering (`prerender = true`) for pages that don't need per-request data
- Use server islands (`server:defer`) to delay rendering of slow/personalized sections without blocking the full page
- Avoid importing heavy client-side libraries in `.astro` frontmatter — they won't be tree-shaken from the server bundle cleanly
- Use `<Image />` from `astro:assets` for automatic image optimization (WebP conversion, lazy loading, width/height)
- Set `Cache-Control` headers via `Astro.response.headers` on pages with infrequently changing data

## Content and Data Fetching

- Fetch data in the frontmatter script with `await fetch(...)` — this always runs server-side
- Keep API base URLs in environment variables (e.g. `API_BASE_URL`) — never hardcode them
- Use `import.meta.env.VAR_NAME` to access environment variables in Astro files
- Variables prefixed with `PUBLIC_` are exposed to the client; all others are server-only
- Never expose `DATABASE_URL`, `OPENAI_API_KEY`, or any secret in a `PUBLIC_` variable

## TypeScript and Type Safety

- Enable TypeScript with `strict: true` in `tsconfig.json`
- Define `interface Props` inside each component's frontmatter for full prop type checking
- Type `locals` in `src/env.d.ts` to get type-safe access to `Astro.locals` across the project
- Use Zod or the built-in Content Collections schema for validating external data shapes

## AI-Assisted Development

- Always use `astro add` when asked to add integrations — not manual `package.json` edits
- Ask AI tools to check the latest docs before using newer APIs (Content Collections, Sessions, Actions) as training data may reflect older patterns
- Set up project rules (like this file) so AI tools follow current Astro conventions consistently
- Use the Astro Docs MCP server (`https://mcp.docs.astro.build/mcp`) to give AI tools real-time access to the latest Astro documentation
- Verify that generated code does not use deprecated patterns (e.g., old `Astro.fetchContent`, `getStaticPaths` misuse in SSR mode)
- Start from an official template (`npm create astro@latest`) rather than building the scaffolding from scratch

## Deployment (Netlify)

- Install `@astrojs/netlify` via `astro add netlify`
- Set `output: 'server'` and `adapter: netlify()` in `astro.config.mjs`
- Define `NODE_VERSION = "22"` in `netlify.toml` under `[build.environment]`
- Set all environment variables in the Netlify UI — never commit `.env` files
- SSR routes become Netlify Functions automatically — no manual serverless configuration required
- Use `netlify.toml` only for build command, publish directory, and Node version; keep everything else in the Netlify dashboard
